// A read of a global that nothing has assigned yet is a runtime error in the
// hosted engine ("undefined variable '<name>'", exit 70). The AOT backend
// reproduces it instead of reading a zeroed slot as `nil` (BACKLOG §6 item 30).
//
// The read is before the assignment, but the assignment exists, so the compiler
// accepts the program (a global with *no* assignment anywhere is a lowering
// error, exit 65, which is a different case). The AOT binary must therefore fail
// at run time with the hosted engine's wording, not read the slot.
//
// `var g = nil;` is deliberately *not* the shape here: assigning `nil` is a
// legitimate value, and the slot has to tell it apart from "never assigned".
print(first);
first = 1;
